If anyone is interested...

As reported on my OpenVMS hobbyist support page,
http://www.djesys.com/vms/hobbyist/support.html the Castlewood
(http://www.castlewood.com/) Orb drive can be expected to display the
"usual 3rd party SCSI" behavior on most later versions of OpenVMS.

However, with the latest firmware update from Castlewood, the Orb
behaves much better with OpenVMS. I tried it with V6.2-1H3 and V7.1-2 so
far (no patches on either version). You will need an MS-DOS system with
a SCSI card in order to apply the firmware update, however.

If not MOUNTed, the Orb drive must be up and spinning when you try to
MOUNT it. After that, it will spin down during periods of inactivity.
However, with the new firmware, it spins back up quickly and does not
NOT go into and out of MntVerify (behaves more like a high-speed
CD-ROM). 

DISMOUNT/UNLOAD *WILL* make the drive spin down. You'll have to do
something manually to spin it back up before you can MOUNT it again.

On the Orb drive I have (external Ultra SCSI), the manual eject button
still does not work, even with the newer firmware. So, this unit may be
defective.

Once INITIALIZEd, the Orb is slightly more capacious than an RZ28. For a
hobbyist system, it may provide an affordable alternative. The drive
itself lists for $179US from CDW (a US Company)
(http://www.cdw.com/shop/search/results.asp?mfg=CST) and the media are
$29.95US for a 2GB cartridge.

By comparison, I found one seller on eBay offering RZ28-VAs for $40/ea.
These aren't removeable and you need a shelf or other suitable equipment
in order to use them; but if you take 'em out of the canisters, you can
mount 'em in just about anything. Depends on what you need, I 'spose.

I haven't yet tried installing OpenVMS to the Orb since the firmware
update. The previous attempt with the original firmware was less than
successful.

-- 
