A stranger in a strange land, indeed!
I understand the sentiment, had the same feeling about the o/s (only in
'82).

Clock speed is a difficult yardstick. Simple comparison: an Intel cpu at 200
MHz
and an Alpha at the same clockrate can do different thing for you.
The Alpha shifts 8 B per cycle in registers, the Intel only 4 B.
OTOH the Alpha is a RISC processor and for more complicated operations
needs more cycles than you might expect.
That's why a VAX can perform quite well, if the application is right.
It's a bit similar to fast cars. A two seater Porsche will get your family
quickly
to another location 200 kms away, provided:
1) the path is unobstructed
2) the path has ni speed limits
3) most importantly: your family fits in it.
Otherwise you might just as well take a bus.

AFAIK the green caddies hold narrow drives. The blue ones hold wide drives.
Then again, I could be wrong.

Hans
