A month or so ago I posted here and C.O.V. trying to get information on
Routeabout firmware upgrades.  Compaq killed the networks.digital.com
sites without warning or forwarding info, so access to the archives there
was lost.  The feedback I got from them was less than helpful at the time
(go talk to DNPG.com, which doesn't do Roamabouts).  DNPG never responded
to my email, nor did Cabletron/Enterasys (who kept the Roamabouts when all
other DEC-derived networking products went to DNPG).  The Enterasys site
only carried very recent firmware for use with IEEE802.11 cards, not for
older DEC 915DS cards.

Well, Enterasys came through big time when I talked to them again.  If you
download the Access Point Manager V6.0 kit from their site, it includes
firmware V2.4 and V5.01 for the DEC Access Points.  V2.4 is the final 
release that supports the older DEC radio cards for 2Mbps throughput.
V5.01 allows use of current Roamabout IEEE802.11 High Rate (11Mbps)
cards in point-to-point or workgroup mode.  The only limitation when
compared to the current Access Point 2000 is that the DEC units cannot
be a central point in a multipoint wireless network (this is a new
feature; originally only two bridges in a point-to-point were allowed).
They _can_ be endpoints that talk to an AP2000 central point.

Per Enterasys support, there is no need for a memory upgrade in the
Access Point, the original 1MB is sufficient (I asked because some
of the docs showed config screens showing 4MB, and I tested one AP
here with a DEC 4MB 32/36 DRAM module, 72 pin, and its working
fine).

So I'm upgrading firmware to V2.4 for now, and keeping an eye open for
a good deal on new Roamabout radio cards; with those a firmware upgrade
will allow us to keep the APs (and hubs they are in) while moving to a
'standard' faster net.  Not too shabby.  I think I'm going to become a 
satisfied customer.

Rich Jordan
rjordan@mcs.net




