Compaq didn't socket the CPU so we have to swap the whole motherboard. (Yet
another reason for the PC folks to laugh at us.)  

Moroever, the cost for your proposed upgrade is about $3500, the full cost 
of the machine minus the disks, memory, and licenses.  I have 10 DS10s
at the moment, 9 running Linux in a beowulf.  Crunch the numbers a little
and you'll find that I can get more CPU oomph in total by buying a small
number of additional machines rather than spending $35000 to move all
existing machines from 466->600 Mhz.  That's only a 30% speed improvement -
I can get more than that much additional horsepower by buying 3 new 600 Mhz 
machines (with memory) for around $12000. So what's my motivation to spend
the extra $23000?  This also tells you that the upgrade price should be 
less than $1200 per machine. At least, that's the price you'll need to
match if you really want a significant number of DS10 users to upgrade. 


Why is it that Compaq does not sell an upgrade that consists of just the
motherboard in a nice sturdy cardboard box?  Cheaper for you, cheaper for
us, less waste, same profit on the part the customer wants, and less
environmental waste.  Add in a trade allowance for returning the original
motherboard and it would be even better.  And it needn't cost Compaq
anything to do so. While I agree it isn't desirable for the end user to try
to desolder and replace the CPU it is certainly cost effective for Compaq
to do it.  Moreover, with a sufficient return flow of such motherboards a
less expensive "class II" upgrade could be offered - which is a 600 Mhz CPU
on a recycled motherboard.  Compaq should certainly be able to desolder a 
chip and solder in a new one for a lot less than the cost of a new 
motherboard!  I bet you already have the correct chip desolder tool on the 
factory floor for removing bad chips from motherboards that fail during 
production.  So there isn't even a capital outlay involved.

Or is it that the 600 Mhz motherboard is really a different beast than the 
466 Mhz variant and Compaq can't recycle the latter either?  If so, please
send your motherboard designers back to school, or at least have them look
at any modern PC motherboard, all of which use socketed CPUs and will run
over a wide range of CPU speeds.  It's bad enough that the Alpha is a niche
market and hence expensive for basic economic reasons, but making it even
more expensive through shortsighted engineering is an unjustified slap in
the face to the customers. 

