> system make use of the dual memory data paths (2x256b)?
> Is this job done through the D-chips (crossbar switch?)?

Of course, a single-CPU config utilizes the two 256Bit memory
banks. As for the memory-Crossbar, the CPU and the Bus content
for memory accesses. Memory access ist controlled by the
Tsunami-chipsets C-chips, which grant access to either the CPU
or the I/O Pchips. However, the two memory-banks provide
enough "headroom" for Dual-configs.

> - From what I've found, the external (L2) cache bus runs at 2/3
> the cpu cycle rate. How does this "match" with synchronous cache
> access?  Is this so for 667 and 833 MHz clocks also?

> - The cache RAM part on the 667MHz cpu board is 233MHz DDR,
> does this mean virtually 466MHz (or is it 117x2)?
> How wide is the data path here?

Well, for the current 21264A's which are used up to 750Mhz,
fairly normal SRAM-technology is used. Only the latest 21264B,
starting at 833Mhz use DDR-SRAM's. The older 21264A's have
a load-to-use latency of 12 cycles towards L2 ( IIRC ) and the
newer 21264B's should have this value greatly reduced.
IIRC, the 21264B uses 417Mhz DDR-SRAMSm providing Data at a
"virtual" rate of 833Mhz. However, due to the natuere of DDR-SRAMS,
adresses are issued at the true 417Mhz clock.


> Now for the be objective department (no flames please :-))
> - Is a Sun E450 really a serious alternativ to the DS20E?
> I don't have any experience with Sun machines, but isn't
> it at least one generation behind? (The quote we've got was
> behind too...)

Given, that you sorted out the OS/Application Software constraints
yourself, the differences btw these Systems are quite significant.
The E450 has a *very good* memory subsystem, using a crossbar as
well. However, the UltraSPARC-II has some limitations wrt
outstanding memory-transactions. Given the comparable performance
of the memory-subsystem itself, i would expect the DS20 to perform
a little better on raw memory-I/O. In addition, the US-II is not
really a match to the Alpha wrt raw processing power. According to
my experience, you could place a 400Mhz US-II at about 0.5-0.7 the
performance of an Alpha at 667Mhz - both given 4Mbytes L2. That
would place a dual-DS20 close to a Quad-E450 in terms of
processing power while both are about equal in terms of memory
bandwidth. Wrt buses, note that the E450 offers four PCI buses,
compared to the two of a DS20 ( IIRC ). Also, the E450 offers
64Bit at 66Mhz on one bus, while the Alpha does 64Bit at 33Mhz.

If you have a fair amount of freedom to choose ( OS, apps ), I 
would most likely opt for the Alpha. The processing power it
offers with only two CPU's, eases application parallelism
constraints and will most likely result in a higher overall
throughput - which of course, would  need to be confirmed by
tests.

If you can spend the money, I would probably go for the new
833Mhz chips, since their DDR-L2 Cache shows an enormous
performance boost in SPEC-2000 figures. If SPEC will somewhat 
reflect your application's performance pattern, the 21264B will
be about the fastest Chip you can get at all.
I can't tell however, if 21264B CPU-daughterboards are already
available for the DS20(E). If you can live with a maximum of
2Gbytes and a single 256bit memory-bus, API's UP2000+ might be
an interesting option. At least they can use the 833Mhz 21264B's
and are less expensive.

As for Sun, you might check the new US-III based systems as
well. The Sun Blade 1000 and the 280R may offer comparable performance
in dual-configs, although I have yet to see one of those systems
( which i will maybe in a few weeks ). 

At my site, i decided to go with a Dual UP2000 ( similar to DS20 ) as
opposed to a E450 and I have absolutely no reason to regret this ;)

Just my $0.02 ;)



