You can link Fibre Channel over an ATM link using CNT boxes -- ask the
Compaq StorageWorks folks for details on the hardware involved.  Do you
already have the fiber link in-place? or just know that dark fiber is
available between your two sites?

> What I'm wondering is this - is anyone in this forum aware of a
> technology that allows Fibre Channel to ride over IP or Ethernet? I'm
> not aware of any gateways of this sort, but I figured I'd ask the
> question.

Cisco and friends are working on SCSI-over-IP standards.  No products
available yet, from what I've heard.

Interesting choice you may face: buy Fibre Channel, which network
experts disparagingly think of as networking gear designed by storage
folks, or SCSI- over-IP, which storage experts disparagingly think of
as storage gear designed by network folks. :-)

> Of course, since it's VMS latency is an issue as well. So something
> hardware-based would be preferable to a software solution.

You might be surprised.  In tests I've seen, the CNT boxes added about
3 milliseconds latency per trip through.  Running an I/O request
through the VMS MSCP server software on a typical Alphaserver these
days adds only 1-2 milliseconds latency.

Considering that you have an existing VMS environment, and considering
the expense and risk involved, I'd strongly recommend doing some real-
world comparisons and tests (or at least much more in-depth
evaluations) of both solutions before you decide, measuring actual
performance, ease of management, and behavior in failover scenarios.  I
wouldn't advise making any prior assumptions about which solution will
be better.

If there are also servers at the remote site which are to be part of
your VMS cluster (most likely, I'm guessing, since data without servers
there is not very useful after failure of your main site), you'll need
a second link in addition to Fibre Channel for the intra-cluster
traffic, since VMS can't send SCS-protocol traffic over Fibre Channel.
If you went the host-based volume shadowing route, you'd only need 1
link for both the cluster and storage traffic, and the link could be
could be DS-3, ATM, FDDI, or 10mbit/Fast/Gigabit Ethernet.
