I've got a customer who has an AlphaStation 500/400 that has developed a
problem that manifests itself as soon as the system is powered-up.

According to the customer, the power-up display is:

ea.e9.e8.e7.e6.e5.e4.
Digital Alpha Station 500/400 Console v6.9-5
     built on oct 10 1998 at 14:22:57
EISA configuration error detected!
you have entered a fix command
fixing NVR Packet @ 0
fixing NVR Identifier @ 280
fixing NVR Data @ 3F8
fixing EISA Data @ 1000
fixing NVR Environment @ BFC
>>>

I haven't seen anything like this before but it certainly looks like the
system has some type of firmware or motherboard problem.  The system
doesn't hold firmware variable settings for either auto_action or
bootdef_dev.  The customer has tried booting from the SCSI CDROM and that
doesn't work also.  A show dev d lists all of the system's SCSI devices
though.  The CDROM drive is an RRD46.  After VMS boots up, the CDROM
produces "medium is offline" errors.

It may be that the CDROM drive is defective and is unrelated to the
firmware messages.  It seems a bit strange to me that EISA is referenced
since I didn't remember the AS500 having EISA slots but maybe that's OK for
the AS500 anyway.  I have thought that maybe the firmware has gotten
corrupt but since I haven't run across that yet I don't know if this would
be the response or if other messages would be seen that would say so
explicitly.

Maybe it's just coincidence that they've got a bad CDROM drive and the
motherboard battery has gone?  Since the system isn't that old it would be
a bit unusual that the battery would've gone dead but I guess that's
possible.  If anyone has seen any firmware problem like this, I'd like to
hear about it.  The fact that the system is 10 timezones away from here has
made diagnosing this a bit difficult.



