In article <3A101BEC.95888D33@lbl.gov>, Tobias Funk <funk@lbl.gov> writes:
:We are using a alpha machine with VMS 6.2. I am writing a fortran
:program and trying to use a system command. I know that you can do this
:wit fortran under unix with the following command:
:value=system("command"). Is there something comparable under VMS?

  Please use the lib$spawn call, it will be easier and more flexible.

  Fortran and C code can be intermixed and you can call system (decc$system,
  since Fortran doesn't add the prefix for you) from Fortran, but C and 
  Fortran have differing assumptions around default argument passing.  C 
  assumes ASCIZ strings, while Fortran assumes ASCID strings.  This means 
  that you would have to create C-format strings in Fortran...

  I'll ask that the response to the version of this question posed over
  in the Ask The Wizard area be cancelled.

