Kurt B. Mundinger <greatlakesrubber-spam-no@home.com> writes:
> I recently acquired the box above.  When I went to plug it in and try to
> determine
> exactly what I have it found a power connection that looks identical to
> the
> normal dec/vax station or vt340  or random pc, EXCEPT this has a "male
> slot"
> opposite the "center" connector.  I made one by slotting a heavy duty
> end from
> an old vt and it fits fine and seats.  But reading this unit is dual
> voltage
> I paused before trying to power this up.  This isn't some kind of 230
> volt 
> connector, is it?  

  As far as I can determine, this is a non-IEC-sanctioned half-assed attempt
at making an "20 Amp" IEC-320 power connector. A *real* 20A IEC connector
looks nothing like this, and is a huge rectangular beast of a connector.

  The issue is that many alleged "IEC-320" cordsets have #18 (or sometimes
smaller) size wires in the cord, which aren't sufficient for higher currents.
If you have a cord with #14 wires, notching it should be safe, or you can
buy a replacement cordset with the notch from a number of the usual places
(DEC spares, Newark Electronics, Mouser, etc.).

  IEC-320 doesn't change connector styles based on input voltage - the unit
could be 120-only, 240-only, auto-sensing, or have a switch. Just make sure
the switch is set the right way (if there is one, it's usually recessed 
near the on-off switch, labeled red with "120/240" markings, and new DEC
gear would have shipped with a yellow tag with the !-trangle symbol and a
note saying either "100-120V" or "200-240V", depending on how the switch
was set.

