Chris Scheers wrote:
> 
> "antonio.carlini" wrote:
> > I am fairly certain, however, that there is at least one
> > place where OpenVMS *could* tell the difference and that
> > is when it is determining the delay value used for short
> > timed loops in a driver macro. Whether OpenVMS chooses
> > to actually note this difference or not I cannot remember.
> 
> Nope, not even there.  The SYSLOA code for the PVAX computes the delay
> values at system startup by testing against a timer.  If your processor
> speed changes, the computed values also change.

That's the point - the code uses a 10ms timer and whips
round a loop counting iterations until the timer ticks.
So the faster box will count more iterations (about 30%
more of them so it should be clear which box you are
running on).

Now if the "upgrade" somehow fails to preserve the diagnostic
timer at 10ms then I can see how this might not work. But if the
diagnostic timer and the main system clock are tied together
(and I expect that they are) then the 10ms must be preserved
otherwise timekeeping would fail.

I do have a KA42-A and -B kicking around at the moment so if I
get a chance I'll load up OpenVMS and peek at EXE$GL_UBDELAY.

Antonio

