[ I *must* be. ]

Basically, I gave this scheme a spin.  Everything works... "almost".
Essentially, the important thing is to run "virtual" copies of samba,
each listening on a virtual IP address.  This is what the "socket
address", "interfaces" and "bind interfaces only" parameters in smb.conf
are for.

Well, that's the theory.  Unfortunately for me, it seems that either
Samba or Tru64 prevent the latter two directives from working.  I get
this message in my log.smb files:

    smbd version 2.0.7 started.
    Copyright Andrew Tridgell 1992-1998
    [2000/11/08 17:05:11, 0] lib/interface.c:(222)
      WARNING: no network interfaces found

which is obviously a problem.  If I remove these two directives, leaving
only the "socket address", and "ifconfig tu0 alias my_service_ip/32",
then smbd listens on that address only, as it should, but nmbd doesn't.
nmbd listens on *.137 (as reported by netstat).  This is, I think, what
confuses clients when connecting.  They either get one nmbd or the
other.  But not both at the same time.  In fact, it looks like whichever
nmbd was started last is the one which responds.  I imagine that, with
both nmbd processes listening on port 137 (all addresses), the behaviour
is undefined...

I'm so near, yet so far.  I presume that this virtual server feature is
working on more mainstrean platforms such as Linux/Intel, since it would
be more widely tested.

Can anyone confirm this?

If not, have I found a Samba bug (i.e. it's not possible to run virtual
servers)?

Has anyone managed to get 2 virtual Samba servers running at the same
time on one Compaq Tru64 box?

