Bob Kaplow wrote:
> 
> Say, does any one have specs on this monitor, or know
> where I can find some infor about them? I just scored
> a pair of them. One goes with a VAX 4000 VLC, but the
> other is extra, and if there is some VGA to RGB adaptor
> that would allow me to use it on one of my peecees, it
> would save me the cost of another monitor. Or is this
> just not practical.

The VRT-19 series are fixed frequency monitors (or pretty
nearly fixed) which uses sync-on-green, meaning that you
will need either a special display card and/or an adapter
cable to use it on PCs or Macs. A search on Google for
either "VRT-19" or "GDM-1960" (or GDM-1961, or whatever
the Sony part number is for your monitor. look on the
label on the back of the case, near the top) should turn
up a number of sources for special video cards and sync
adapter cables. Some of the places I frequently go are:

Software Integrators at <http://www.si87.com>

Griffin Technology at <http://www.nashville.net/~griffin>

Both companies have online monitor spec databases as
well as afew products that will help you to use that
beautiful 19" display.

You should check to see which sub-model of the VRT-19
you have. I have seen at least two different models of
the VRT-19: VRT-19 HA and VRT-19 DA, which are subtly
different in their specs. The specs that I have for
the two models are:

VRT-19 DA: Sony model# GDM-1960
resolution: 1024x768
H.freq: 54KHz
V.freq: 66Hz

VRT-19 HA: Sony model# GDM-1961
resolution: 1152x870 or 1280x1024
H.freq: 68KHz, 70KHz, or 77KHz
V.freq: 66.5Hz, 72.6Hz, or 75Hz

For the HA sub-model, the online databases only show
a single resolution and frequency set, but I was told
via e-mail that the actual monitor specs from DEC have
more options. Since I am running an HA as one of the
alternate resolution/frequency options, I assume that
the e-mail was correct. Never the less, this minor
variability in resolution/frequency settings doesn't
make the VRT-19 models able to function as true multi-
frequency monitors: they won't work with the standard
output from a PC video card in 640x480 mode.

A few years ago it was too expensive to outfit a PC to
handle fixed frequency monitors, but you could hook one
up to a Mac with relatively little diffictulty (Macs will
remember what resolution you last had them set at and
start up in the same resolution next time, rather than
always trying to boot up in some wonky low-res mode).
Today, however, there are video cards for PCs that will
handle fixed freq. and are still reasonably priced.
(Software Integrators has two such cards for between
$100-$150).

- Jeff Dutky


