jason andrade <jason@azure.dstc.edu.au> writes:
> wow.  i have a couple of infoserver 150s.. i never realised they were
> (albeit weird) microvax 3100s, though i knew they had a VAX cpu.  is
> it actually possible to run VMS on them ?

  Not all InfoServers are based on the 3100's, and I'm not that familiar
with the model numbering. The ones in the big boxes are definitely 3100's,
while the smaller boxes (that look like slightly-oversized Ethernet print
servers) aren't 3100's and I don't think they have VAX CPU's at all.

  If you have one of the big ones, you should be able to turn it into a
3100 by changing the boot ROMs to the appropriate ones for the matching 
VAX. However, as someone (Hoff?) said, why do this when a) the ROMs aren't
available any more, and b) you could sell the InfoServer, buy a 3100, and
have money left over, since the InfoServers are more valuable.

  [Without changing the boot ROMs, you have 2 problems: 1) doesn't know
how to boot VMS from local disk, and 2) the system ID is wrong and you'd
get "unknown CPU model" or somesuch at boot time.]

