Unfortunatly the Unix systems I have access to, currently do not have 
Fortran installed. So I can only help by outlining wht i would have done.

Anyway, on VMS the fortran program will generate a 'variable length'
record file with maximum record size 2044 where each file record is
a part (segment) of the application data record. To implement this,
there is a header value for each segment (= rms record) which indicates
whether this segment is the first (1), middle (0), last(2) or only(3) one 
needed to build an application record. Use DUMP/RECORD to verify.

>  (2) Binary ftp the file testi.d from Open VMS/AXP to True64 UNIX

That means you have send the record length 'overhead' words (in the
test case values 2044, 2044 and 14 over to Unix. DOes it know what
to do with that? Use DUMP/BLOC to verify. On Unix use some flavor of 'od'

>  (3) Read the file using Fortran "unformatted read" on True64 UNIX (read.f)
>      (OSF1 V4.0 1229 alpha, DIGITAL Fortran 77 V5.2-171-428BH)

Here I'd suggest you run the file creation program on Unix and dump (od)
the resulting file to understand the implemenation. Actually, in reality
i would probably do a binary transfer to VMS and DUMP it there.

Once you know how the Unix file should look like, I am sure you can find
a way on VMS to create it just so. Maybe all it takes is FTP in text mode
(I doubt it, considering the binary data). Maybe it takes a $CONVERT to
STREAM_LF (again, unlikely if there is binary data, but maybe). 

The FOrtran documentation on Unix should also explain what data is expected.

hth,
