: By the way, reading the SPD, it appears that TurboChannel machines, such
: as the 3000/300LX, are no longer supported by Tru64 Unix. I'm not sure
: whether the "retirement" came in 5.0 or 5.1, but none of the
: TurboChannel machines are listed in the current SPD.

The "retirement" came in 5.1 (maybe even 5.0A, I'd have to double-check).
In reality, the "retirement" has been coming in phases since 4.0C/D, first
by dropping Open3D support for the high-end framebuffers and then in 5.0 by
dropping support for all but the basic 8-bit framebuffers.  5.1 drops *all*
TurboChannel support, thus ending the support for any of the 2000 or 3000
series boxes.

That said, on the limited configuration of boxes that 5.0 will run on, it
runs pretty well from what I can tell, provided you don't starve it of RAM
(which has always been a caveat on Tru64\Digital UNIX\OSF/1).

The original poster will be most interested to know that his hardware *will*
run 5.0 with the built-in framebuffer, but as the later poster hinted will
not run 5.1.

: Of course, VMS still supports these machines.

VMS still supports almost *everything*, doesn't it?  Then again, that's one
of the major plusses in its favor...

Chris

